 {"id":"gcal-borna-20260619T140000","end_time":"2026-06-19T15:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHDhKnFE=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":null,"tags":[]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-borna-20260616T140000","end_time":"2026-06-16T15:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHJxVsnc=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Covered statistical mechanics foundations: counting microstates (Omega = 8C4 for 8 particles split between two halves), the Boltzmann factor (P_i proportional to e^(-E_i/kT)), and two direct applications -- the barometric formula (P = P0 * e^(-Mgh/RT), scale height kT/Mg) and the Maxwell-Boltzmann energy distribution (f(KE) proportional to KE * e^(-KE/kT)). Analyzed a two-level system: computed Boltzmann ratio Z = e^(-(E1-E0)/kT) giving populations 80%/20%. Noted that kT approximately 1/40 eV at 300 K is much less than typical transition energies (~3 eV), so excited states are rarely thermally populated. Introduced thermodynamic temperature T = dE/dS and Boltzmann entropy S = k_B * Sum(P_i * log(P_i)).","tags":["statistical mechanics","Boltzmann distribution","Maxwell-Boltzmann","entropy","partition function","barometric formula","thermodynamics"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-india-g9jl3iavjvm9co41vbl8rc9sp8","end_time":"2026-06-12T15:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHH6_f_s=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Explored orbital mechanics and astronomical scales. Sketched the elliptical orbit of a planet around the Sun (Sun at a focus) and marked the \"winter\" position to talk through how orbital geometry relates to the seasons. Built up a years-to-seconds unit conversion (60 × 60 × 24 × 365) and used it to turn a duration of about 3.09×10^17 seconds into roughly 9.5×10^9 years. Practiced scientific-notation arithmetic along the way — multiplying and dividing powers of ten and handling negative exponents (5^-1 = 1/5) — and wrote out the speed of light (2.99792458×10^5 km/s) for a light-travel / large-distance estimate.","tags":["orbital mechanics","elliptical orbit","seasons","speed of light","unit conversion","scientific notation","powers of ten","astronomical scales"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-leo-20260610T190000","end_time":"2026-06-10T20:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHIUvJO0=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Applied Taylor series to practical approximations. First motivated series via kinematics: given x(54s) and v(54s), estimated x(54.1s) ~ 104.016 m using x(t+dt) ~ x(t) + v(t)*dt (first-order Taylor). Then derived the binomial approximation (1+x)^n ~ 1+nx for small x, including e = lim(n->inf)(1+1/n)^n as a special case. Worked through bound-formula estimates without a calculator: e.g., sqrt(65) = sqrt(64*(1+1/64)) ~ 8*(1+1/128) = 8.0625. Practiced on a problem set covering e^x, sqrt, and related approximations.","tags":["Taylor series","kinematics","binomial approximation","linear approximation","number e","limits","estimation","calculus"]}, 
 {"id":"cal-20489317-india","end_time":"2026-06-09T17:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHJ3teeM=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"First full session (3 hours). Covered unit conversions systematically: 1 in = 2.54 cm, 12 in = 1 ft, 1 m = 3.28 ft, 5 km ~ 3 miles; computed area conversion from m^2 to ft^2. Worked through speed/distance problems including converting 36 km/h to mph and estimating a professional cyclist's speed from video (wheel diameter ~0.97 m, 1 m/s ~ 2 mph). Discussed the Vancouver car-rental unit-error problem (/usr/bin/bash.25/km x 36,482 km -> ,000+ bill: likely a miles/km confusion). Practiced proportional reasoning (scaling dots and time by different factors) and interpreted a photoelectric-effect-style graph to evaluate claims about relative experimental error.","tags":["unit conversions","dimensional analysis","estimation","speed","proportional reasoning","metric system","graph interpretation","Fermi problems"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-leo-20260609T003000","end_time":"2026-06-09T01:30:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHJWIbrQ=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Working through Blue Morin, started on Taylor series","tags":["Taylor series"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-leo-20260605T003000","end_time":"2026-06-05T01:30:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHJ6teXM=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Derived Taylor series for sin and cos from first principles. Started with the unit circle and the inequality theta > sin(theta) to motivate sin(theta) = theta - theta^3/6 + theta^5/120 + ..., then established lim(theta->0) sin(theta)/theta = 1. Derived cos(theta) = 1 - theta^2/2 + theta^4/24 by assuming the form a + b*theta + c*theta^2 + ... and solving for coefficients using cos(0)=1 and the Pythagorean identity. Applied the cosine series to pendulum potential energy U = -mgl*cos(theta), showing the small-angle approximation gives U ~ (1/2)kx^2 -- i.e., the pendulum behaves like a spring for small oscillations.","tags":["Taylor series","trig functions","sinθ/θ limit","small-angle approximation","pendulum","unit circle","Pythagorean identity","calculus"]}, 
 {"id":"47b36a5c-fcd5-4034-8905-b189ab3a739e","end_time":"2026-06-02T20:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"uXjVHMRK-js=","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"30-min intro meeting","tags":["intro","first session"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-borna-20260526T140000","end_time":"2026-05-29T15:11:14+00:00","miro_board_id":null,"miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"linear and circular polarization, begin looking at Tokyo 2023 experiment on birefringence","tags":["polarization","circular polarization2","optics"]}, 
 {"id":"gcal-borna-20260527T140000","end_time":"2026-05-29T14:46:56+00:00","miro_board_id":null,"miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Beginning study of circuits. Concept of impedance, equivalent circuits, resistivity, power dissipation","tags":["circuits","thevenin theorem","impedance","equivalent circuits","resistivity","power"]}, 
 {"id":"leo-session-2026-05-28","end_time":"2026-05-28T01:00:00+00:00","miro_board_id":"034a1c3e-b944-4e12-bdac-ca6a6c49bb61","miro_pdf_url":null,"summary":"Constant-speed motion on a curved path: analyzed which x(t) and y(t) plot pairs are consistent with |v|=1 m/s. Used Vₓ²+Vy²=v² to verify — e.g. Vₓ=0.8, Vy=0.6 gives |v|=1 (a 3-4-5 triple scaled down). Computed circular motion speed V=2πR/T (R=10m, T=10s → V=2π m/s). Drew velocity vector decomposition triangle. Illustrated displacement vs. arc length using a 6-8-10 right triangle. Also introduced the epsilon-delta definition of a limit: lim_{x→a} f(x)=L means for all ε>0, there exists δ>0 such that 0<|x-a|<δ implies |f(x)-L|<ε.","tags":["kinematics","velocity-components","speed","circular-motion","Pythagorean-theorem","displacement","limits","epsilon-delta"]}]